WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.367 --> 00:00:02.967
(upbeat music)

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.500
Hi everyone.
I am Priyanka Vergadia,

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.667
developer advocate
on the Google Cloud team,

00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:12.733
and today I’m going to show you
how to use Vertex AI

00:00:12.733 --> 00:00:14.667
and help a friend get into yoga.

00:00:15.633 --> 00:00:18.100
We are building
a yoga pose classification model.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.133
Let's get started.

00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:22.067
First thing we need is the data.

00:00:22.967 --> 00:00:25.167
For this step, my friend Sarah and I

00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:27.633
collected a bunch of images
from our yoga practice

00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:30.100
and uploaded them
into the managed data sets.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.700
As you see here,
the images are labeled in five categories.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.667
I can even upload unlabeled images
and label them.

00:00:38.433 --> 00:00:39.833
We can analyze our data set

00:00:39.833 --> 00:00:43.100
to see that we have
enough examples of each categories.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.300
Now we are ready to train our model.

00:00:47.133 --> 00:00:50.833
In here, I’m selecting
AutoML for training method.

00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:54.333
You can also train a model
to deploy on the edge or on premise,

00:00:54.333 --> 00:00:58.133
or use custom training
if you’re writing your own custom model code.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.900
Now we define our model.

00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:07.167
AutoML automatically splits the data
into training, validation, and test,

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:09.067
but we can change it if we want to.

00:01:10.833 --> 00:01:12.433
In compute and pricing,

00:01:12.433 --> 00:01:16.133
we get to select how many node hours
we want to train the model.

00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:19.533
At this point,
we wait for the model to train.

00:01:20.533 --> 00:01:24.667
Once it is trained we see it in the Model tab
and can evaluate the model.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.833
We can see our model's average precision.

00:01:28.833 --> 00:01:30.700
It ranges from zero to one,

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.633
where a higher value
indicates a higher quality model.

00:01:36.333 --> 00:01:38.133
We can see that the confusion matrix

00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:41.967
helps us understand where our model
is confusing two results.

00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:46.100
Now that we have evaluated our model,
it’s time to deploy it to an endpoint.

00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:51.867
We can split traffic between two models
deployed on the same endpoint if needed.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:56.600
Allocate the number of nodes
needed for this endpoint.

00:01:56.933 --> 00:02:01.100
Depending on where your users are,
select a region to deploy your endpoint.

00:02:01.767 --> 00:02:04.633
And that's it.
Hit deploy and we have an endpoint.

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:09.533
Now we can test our endpoint and the model
by uploading an image.

00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:12.133
And yay, it works.

00:02:12.133 --> 00:02:15.200
You can also make predictions using the SDK.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.933
If this has piqued your interest in Vertex AI
and you want to learn more,

00:02:18.933 --> 00:02:21.567
head over to the documentation
linked on screen.

00:02:21.567 --> 00:02:22.600
Thanks for watching.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.200
(upbeat music)

